C = [
("h1", "2. Isaiah 1: The Crowded House"),
("p", "Claim: the first chapter of Isaiah is the longest and plainest indictment of worship in the prophets, and it condemns neither the offerings nor the appointed times. It condemns a people whose hands are full of blood coming to a house the LORD has already said he is weary of seeing them tread. It names the remedy in nine commands, offers to reason with them, and ends with a purged and restored city. The order of worship is the setting of the charge from the first verse to the last."),
("sub", "2.1 The setting: Judah and Jerusalem"),
("p", "The chapter says whom it is about before it says anything about worship. “The vision of Isaiah the son of Amoz, which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem in the days of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, kings of Judah” (Isaiah 1:1). The LORD has “nourished and brought up children, and they have rebelled against me” (Isaiah 1:2). The nation is “laden with iniquity,” a people who “have forsaken the LORD” (Isaiah 1:4). The land is desolate and the cities burned (Isaiah 1:7). Verse 9 says that “except the LORD of hosts had left unto us a very small remnant,” Judah would have been like Sodom and Gomorrah."),
("p", "The next verse takes the names from the verse before. “Hear the word of the LORD, ye rulers of Sodom; give ear unto the law of our God, ye people of Gomorrah” (Isaiah 1:10). Judah’s rulers are called rulers of Sodom, and the prophet’s message is called the law of our God. The word the prophet speaks is set beside the law, not against it. What follows is not a new teaching about what God wants in place of what he commanded. It is the application of what God commanded to those who handle it wrongly."),
("sub", "2.2 The multitude of your sacrifices"),
("p", "The first question is about quantity. “To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me? saith the LORD: I am full of the burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of fed beasts; and I delight not in the blood of bullocks, or of lambs, or of he goats” (Isaiah 1:11). Three things in the sentence are worth marking. The sacrifices are called “your sacrifices unto me,” offerings brought to him. The sentence speaks of a multitude: the problem is the pile, and the idea in the pile. And the verb is “delight,” the same verb Samuel used when he asked whether the LORD had as great delight in burnt offerings as in obeying his voice (1 Samuel 15:22). The comparison of rank is in the background again. The LORD says he has more than enough of these and delights in none of them as they are brought."),
("p", "The second question is about the one who requires. “When ye come to appear before me, who hath required this at your hand, to tread my courts?” (Isaiah 1:12). The question can be misread as a denial that the LORD commanded anything. It is not that. The Law had appointed the courts, the offerings and the coming to appear. The question is who has required this at their hand: this treading, this coming, in this state. The sentence asks whether the LORD requires of men the visit of men with blood on their hands. The courts are “my courts.” The LORD does not disown the house. He speaks of it as his own."),
("sub", "2.3 The appointed times named and refused"),
("p", "Verses 13 and 14 run through the calendar. “Bring no more vain oblations; incense is an abomination unto me; the new moons and sabbaths, the calling of assemblies, I cannot away with; it is iniquity, even the solemn meeting. Your new moons and your appointed feasts my soul hateth: they are a trouble unto me; I am weary to bear them” (Isaiah 1:13, 14). The oblation, the incense, the new moons, the sabbaths, the assemblies and the feasts are the Law’s own list. The LORD calls them vain, an abomination, a trouble and a weariness in the hands of this people. The phrase “your appointed feasts” is the plainest evidence in the passage that these are the appointed times. The word “appointed” belongs to the Law’s own vocabulary for the feasts, and it is used here against the way the people keep them."),
("p", "The text gives the reason in verse 13: “it is iniquity, even the solemn meeting.” The meeting and the iniquity are joined. The LORD says he cannot away with the assembly of those who bring iniquity into it. The prophet is not saying that assembling is iniquity. He is saying that a solemn meeting held by those who hold on to wickedness is itself made iniquity by the joining. The next verses say what the iniquity is."),
("sub", "2.4 The hands and the prayers"),
("p", "“And when ye spread forth your hands, I will hide mine eyes from you: yea, when ye make many prayers, I will not hear: your hands are full of blood” (Isaiah 1:15). The offering is joined here to prayer, and the LORD hides his eyes from the one and does not hear the other. The reason is in the last clause: “your hands are full of blood.” The hands that are spread forth in prayer and that bring the sacrifice are the hands that have shed it in the street. Verse 17 names the shape of the sin in the city: the oppressed, the fatherless and the widow are the ones whose cause is neglected, and verse 23 says that the princes “judge not the fatherless, neither doth the cause of the widow come unto them.”"),
("p", "The text offers two things the prophet could have said and did not say. He did not say that the hands should stop offering. He did not say that prayer is useless. He said that these hands and these prayers are not heard, and then he told them what to do about it."),
("sub", "2.5 Wash you: the nine commands"),
("p", "Verses 16 and 17 are the turn of the chapter. “Wash you, make you clean; put away the evil of your doings from before mine eyes; cease to do evil; learn to do well; seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow” (Isaiah 1:16, 17). Count the verbs: wash, make clean, put away, cease, learn, seek, relieve, judge, plead. They are commands of conduct, and none of them is a command to stop sacrificing or to stop keeping the feasts. The washing is a washing of the doings “from before mine eyes.” The prophet joins the language of cleansing to the language of justice: what makes the worshiper clean is the putting away of evil and the doing of justice for those with no one to speak for them."),
("p", "The remedy is also an offer. “Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool” (Isaiah 1:18). The LORD invites argument and promises cleansing. Then the two ways are set out: “If ye be willing and obedient, ye shall eat the good of the land: but if ye refuse and rebel, ye shall be devoured with the sword: for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it” (Isaiah 1:19, 20). The conditional word is the one Volume 1 traced in the prophets’ rule for announced judgment and blessing. The people’s response decides what the LORD does."),
("sub", "2.6 The city purged and restored"),
("p", "The chapter ends with a verdict on the city and a promise for it. “How is the faithful city become an harlot! it was full of judgment; righteousness lodged in it; but now murderers” (Isaiah 1:21). The silver has become dross and the wine is mixed with water (Isaiah 1:22). The LORD will “turn my hand upon thee, and purely purge away thy dross, and take away all thy tin” (Isaiah 1:25). Then he will “restore thy judges as at the first, and thy counsellors as at the beginning: afterward thou shalt be called, The city of righteousness, the faithful city” (Isaiah 1:26). The chapter ends where it began, with Zion: “Zion shall be redeemed with judgment, and her converts with righteousness” (Isaiah 1:27)."),
("p", "The sentence of the chapter on the house is therefore a purging and a restoring, and the pattern is the one the earlier voices set. The offerings are not the dross. The judges, the counsellors and the princes who love gifts are. When the city is restored it is called the city of righteousness, and nothing in the chapter says that the house, the feasts or the offerings will not be part of that city. The chapter is silent on that, and the volume does not fill the silence. It says only that nothing in Isaiah 1 proposes an end of the order, and that the whole chapter is addressed to Judah and Jerusalem, in the days of four named kings."),
("ctbl", "Isaiah 1 in five movements.", [
    ("Verses", "What is said", "What it shows"),
    ("1:1–9", "The vision concerns Judah and Jerusalem; the nation is desolate; a very small remnant remains", "The audience is Judah in the days of named kings; the setting is judgment already begun"),
    ("1:10–15", "Sacrifices, oblations, incense, new moons, sabbaths, assemblies and feasts are refused; prayer is not heard; the hands are full of blood", "The list is the Law’s own; the LORD refuses it from these hands, and calls the courts “my courts”"),
    ("1:16–17", "Wash you; cease to do evil; learn to do well; seek judgment; relieve, judge, plead", "Nine commands of conduct; none is a command to stop the offerings"),
    ("1:18–20", "Come and reason; scarlet to snow; willing and obedient, or refuse and rebel", "A conditional word, spoken by the mouth of the LORD, to a people that can turn"),
    ("1:21–31", "The faithful city is an harlot; the LORD will purge the dross, restore the judges, redeem Zion with judgment", "The sentence is a purging and a restoring of the city, not an abolishing of its worship"),
  ], [0.14, 0.46, 0.4]),
("sub", "2.7 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 1:10 calls the prophet’s message the law of our God; the condemnation is spoken as an application of the Law, not as its replacement.",
    "The things the LORD refuses in verses 11–14, the sacrifices, oblations, incense, new moons, sabbaths, assemblies and feasts, are the Law’s own list; the LORD calls the feasts “your appointed feasts” and the house “my courts.”",
    "The reason is stated in the passage: “it is iniquity, even the solemn meeting,” and “your hands are full of blood.”",
    "The remedy is nine commands of conduct and an offer to reason; no command in the chapter is to stop offering.",
    "The chapter ends with the LORD purging the city and restoring it, and it says nothing of abolishing the order within it.",
  ]),
]
